        <lowerValue xmi:id="_18_4_1_e120334_1499417195047_36531_37619" xmi:uuid="30492265-3913-43a5-a464-8a1d01c4164a" xmi:type="uml:LiteralInteger"/>
        <association xmi:idref="_18_4_1_e120334_1499417193746_278058_27308"/>
      </ownedEnd>
    </packagedElement>
    <packagedElement xmi:id="_18_4_1_e120334_1499417193224_512053_23813" xmi:uuid="65c1672b-ba7c-4975-82be-d0436d499ff8" xmi:type="uml:Class">
      <ownedComment xmi:id="_18_0_5_45e01bd_1455704527005_392002_42609" xmi:uuid="09eee97f-45be-4087-b7ba-67b54d64d578" xmi:type="uml:Comment">
        <annotatedElement xmi:idref="_18_4_1_e120334_1499417193224_512053_23813"/>
        <body>A Requirement is an essential characteristic, condition, or capability that shall be met or exceeded by a system or a component to satisfy a contract, standard, specification, or other formally imposed document. &lt;note&gt; The term "requirement" is used here in the sense that term is used in systems engineering and similar industrial domains. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;note&gt; There may be many versions of a Requirement through RequirementVersion. There may also be more than one domain-specific view of a given RequirementVersion (using the RequirementView entity). The Requirement entity is simply a placeholder for holding a unique requirement. Most associations and properties could be defined at the Requirement, RequirementVersion or RequirementView level - that is in the context of a domain. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;example&gt;A requirement may be identified as "NOx emissions requirement", and uniquely identified as "Req2". &lt;/example&gt;&lt;note&gt; Systems engineering tools and organizations may use differing identification mechanisms. Multiple identifiers may be managed in Requirement using the id attribute.&lt;/note&gt;</body>
      </ownedComment>
      <name>Requirement</name>
